---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/deck';
import { deckMaterials } from '../lib/deck';
import { int } from '../lib/format';

// 常见露台尺寸：板长取不小于露台长度的标准长度
const STOCK = [8, 10, 12, 16, 20];
const sizes: [number, number][] = [
  [10, 10],
  [12, 12],
  [16, 12],
  [16, 16],
  [20, 12],
  [20, 16],
  [20, 20],
];
const rows = sizes.map(([l, w]) => {
  const board = STOCK.find((s) => s >= l) ?? 20;
  const r = deckMaterials({ lengthFt: l, widthFt: w, boardWidthIn: 5.5, gapIn: 0.125, boardLengthFt: board, joistSpacingIn: 16, wastePct: 10 });
  return { l, w, board, ...r };
});
---

<CalcPage
  slug="deck-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Deck Calculator: Boards, Joists & Screws for Your Deck"
  description="Free deck calculator. Enter your deck size, board width, gap, and joist spacing to get the number of deck boards, joists, rim joists, and deck screws."
  h1="Deck Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Count the deck boards, joists, rim joists, and screws for a rectangular deck, based on your board size, gap, and joist
    spacing.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate deck boards</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the number of rows: deck width in inches ÷ (board width + gap). Round up; the last board may be ripped narrower.</li>
    <li>Find boards per row: deck length ÷ board length, rounded up. Longer decks need a butt joint in each row.</li>
    <li>Multiply rows × boards per row and add about 10% for cuts and rejects.</li>
    <li>Joists: deck length in inches ÷ joist spacing, plus one.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: a 16 × 12 ft deck</h2>
  <p>
    With 5.5-inch boards and a ⅛-inch gap, each row takes 5.625 inches: 144 ÷ 5.625 = 25.6, so 26 rows. One 16 ft board
    covers each row, and 10% extra brings it to <strong>29 boards</strong>. At 16 inches on center the frame needs 13
    joists, each 12 ft long, plus two 16 ft rim joists. Two screws per board at every joist is 676 screws.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Deck boards for common deck sizes</h2>
  <p>5.5 in boards, ⅛ in gap, joists 16 in on center, 10% extra. Board length is the shortest stock length that spans the deck.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Deck size</th><th class="num">Sq ft</th><th class="num">Boards</th><th class="num">Board length</th><th class="num">Joists</th><th class="num">Screws</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <tr><td>{r.l} × {r.w} ft</td><td class="num">{r.areaFt2}</td><td class="num">{r.boards}</td><td class="num">{r.board} ft</td><td class="num">{r.joists}</td><td class="num">{int(r.screws)}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="board-sizes">Nominal vs. actual deck board sizes</h2>
  <p>Lumber is sold by nominal size, but the calculator needs the actual width.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Board</th><th>Actual size</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>5/4 × 6 decking</td><td>1 × 5.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 × 6</td><td>1.5 × 5.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 × 4</td><td>1.5 × 3.5 in</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Composite decking</td><td>Usually about 5.5 in wide; check the product sheet</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="joist-spacing">What joist spacing do you need?</h2>
  <p>
    Sixteen inches on center is standard under wood boards laid square to the joists. Many composite and PVC boards call
    for 12 inches, and diagonal decking needs closer spacing too, because the boards span farther between supports. The
    decking manufacturer's installation guide and your local code set the limit, so check both before you build.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-included">What this calculator doesn't include</h2>
  <p>
    Posts, beams, ledger board, footings, railings, and stairs depend on your deck's height and local code. For footing
    holes, the <a href="/concrete-calculator/">concrete calculator</a> has a post-hole mode with bag counts. If you are
    fencing the yard around the deck, the <a href="/fence-calculator/">fence calculator</a> handles posts and pickets.
  </p>

  <p>
    Pricing framing lumber at a yard that sells by volume? <a href="/board-foot-calculator/">Convert it to board feet</a>. For steps down to the
    yard, <a href="/stair-calculator/">lay out the deck stairs and stringers</a> from the deck height.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How many deck screws do I need?</h3>
  <p>
    Two per board at every joist. For a deck with 16-inch joist spacing that works out to roughly 350 screws per 100
    square feet, which is why deck screws are sold in large boxes.
  </p>
  <h3>How big a gap should I leave between deck boards?</h3>
  <p>
    Kiln-dried and composite boards typically get ⅛ to ¼ inch, per the manufacturer. Wet pressure-treated lumber is often
    butted tight, because it shrinks and opens a gap as it dries.
  </p>
  <h3>Which way should deck boards run?</h3>
  <p>
    Perpendicular to the joists, which usually means parallel to the house. Enter the length along the boards as
    <em>Deck length</em>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rows = width ÷ (board width + gap), rounded up. Boards = rows × boards per row, plus the extra you choose.</li>
    <li>Joists = length ÷ spacing + 1, each as long as the deck is wide. Rim joists are two boards the length of the deck.</li>
    <li>Screws = rows × joists × 2. Results assume a simple rectangle without picture framing.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
